# A shared-world server — the thing behind a Minecraft-like game.
#
# WHY THIS SHAPE, and why it is the actor story's real test rather than a
# second matching engine.
#
# `matching_engine.tuck` is ONE actor: a serialized stream into a single
# owner. That is a real deployment, but it exercises none of what people
# actually mean by "microservice-like actors" — there is no routing, no
# fan-out to shards, no fan-in barrier, and no message that is produced BY
# an actor rather than by the feed. This has all four:
#
#     ingress (plain fns; `[io]` socket tasks in a deployment)
#            |
#        Gateway          validate, then route by world coordinate
#        /  |  \  \
#   Shard0 Shard1 Shard2 Shard3    each owns a strip of the world
#        \  |  /  /
#        Journal          the append-only change log, and the drain barrier
#
# That is how every shared-world server is actually deployed — a gateway
# that owns no world state, N zone servers that each own a disjoint slice of
# it, and a log that everyone writes to. The slicing is not an optimisation:
# it is what makes the edits safe to apply in parallel at all, because two
# shards can never touch the same column.
#
# THE WORK PER MESSAGE IS A LIGHTING UPDATE, which is the honest choice. In
# a voxel world the expensive part of "place a block" has never been storing
# the block; it is re-propagating sky light around it, because light is a
# flood and a single edit changes a neighbourhood. Here that is a bounded
# relaxation over a window of columns — real work that scales with the edit,
# not a counter bump, so the handler costs more than the handoff.
#
# WHAT TUCK COULD NOT EXPRESS, written down because it shaped the file:
#
#   * The shards are four hand-written singletons. An actor is a
#     compile-time singleton with no reference type, so `Shard[i]` is not a
#     thing that can be indexed and the router cannot be a loop — it is a
#     `match` with one arm per shard. Same wall `benches/savina/threadring`
#     hit; here it is worse, because the natural deployment knob for a world
#     server is "how many zone servers", and that knob is a source edit.
#
#   * Every queue is sized above the WHOLE burst. With no backpressure and a
#     silent drop on a full mailbox (KNOWN-BUGS-EVENTS.md EV-11) a pipeline
#     is only as safe as its most-loaded stage, and a dropped `flush` does
#     not merely lose an edit — it hangs the drain forever. A one-actor
#     program can paper over that with one big queue. A pipeline cannot: the
#     Gateway takes the full burst AND the Journal takes one message per
#     edit, so both need it, and a shard needs its share. This is the
#     clearest case yet for EV-11 being a design gap rather than a nit.

import scheduler
import seq
import console
import str

# The world is 1024 columns wide, four shards of 256. Heights are block
# counts above sea level, as they are in a real chunk's heightmap — an
# integer, never a float.
const SPAN = 256
const SHARDS = 4
const MAXLIGHT = 15
const TORCH = 14

fn zeroed({n: int}) -> Seq[int]:
  var out = [0]           # seeded: an empty list has no element type (TK-TY20)
  var i = 1
  for i < n:
    out = {items: out, value: 0} push
    i = i + 1
  return out

# --- lighting -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The slice is UNDERGROUND — a corridor, not a surface. That is deliberate
# and it is where the cost actually is: open sky reaches every column
# equally, so a surface light model is local and a sweep over it changes
# nothing. Underground the only light is what someone placed, so a torch
# lights the tunnel for fifteen columns either way, a placed block casts a
# shadow past itself, and BREAKING a torch darkens everything it was
# lighting. Light removal being the expensive case is not an artifact of
# this file; it is the thing voxel engines are known for.

# One shard's slice of the world. Held as a single record for the same
# reason the order book is: it keeps every handler one line, which is also
# how a real zone server is written — one apply path, the coordinate as a
# parameter.
type Slice:
  height: Seq[int]      # how much each column blocks
  lum:    Seq[int]      # what each column emits — a torch, or nothing
  light:  Seq[int]      # what actually reaches it
  edits:  int
  relit:  int
  denied: int

fn freshSlice({cols: int}) -> Slice:
  let h = {n: cols} zeroed
  let e = {n: cols} zeroed
  let l = {n: cols} zeroed
  return {height: h, lum: e, light: l, edits: 0, relit: 0, denied: 0} Slice

# A relaxation in progress. `step` is +1 for the left-to-right pass and -1
# for the right-to-left one, so the two passes are the same code.
type Flood:
  height: Seq[int]
  lum:    Seq[int]
  light:  Seq[int]
  i:      int
  relit:  int

# Light at a column is the brightest of what it emits and what leaks in from
# behind — one level dimmer for the distance, and dimmer again by whatever
# the column itself blocks. A solid column stops the leak, which is what
# makes a placed block cast a shadow rather than merely be dark.
#
# `keep` is what makes two passes exact. The first pass ASSIGNS, discarding
# the old value, which is what lets a removed torch actually go dark; the
# second pass keeps what the first found and maxes the other direction in.
# Assign-then-max is the 1-D distance transform, and getting it backwards
# gives light that can brighten but never dim.
fn relaxOne({f: Flood, step: int, keep: bool}) -> Flood:
  var l = f.light
  let src = if keep and l[f.i] > f.lum[f.i]: l[f.i] else: f.lum[f.i]
  let leak = l[f.i - step] - 1 - f.height[f.i]
  let want = if leak > src: leak else: src
  let hit = if want != l[f.i]: 1 else: 0
  l[f.i] = want
  return {height: f.height, lum: f.lum, light: l, i: f.i + step, relit: f.relit + hit} Flood

# One directional pass from `at` to `to`.
fn pass({f: Flood, at: int, to: int, step: int, keep: bool}) -> Flood:
  var w = {height: f.height, lum: f.lum, light: f.light, i: at, relit: f.relit} Flood
  for (to - w.i) * step >= 0:
    w = {f: w, step: step, keep: keep} relaxOne
  return w

# The window an edit can possibly have changed: light falls off at least one
# level per column, so nothing beyond MAXLIGHT columns can be affected.
# Clamped to the shard, which is also where the authority ends — a shard may
# not read its neighbour's columns, and the seam that leaves is the last
# note at the bottom of this file.
fn relight({sl: Slice, c: int, cols: int}) -> Slice:
  let lo = if c - MAXLIGHT > 1: c - MAXLIGHT else: 1
  let hi = if c + MAXLIGHT < cols - 2: c + MAXLIGHT else: cols - 2
  let a = {height: sl.height, lum: sl.lum, light: sl.light, i: lo, relit: 0} Flood
  let r = {f: a, at: lo, to: hi, step: 1, keep: false} pass
  let b = {f: r, at: hi, to: lo, step: -1, keep: true} pass
  return {height: sl.height, lum: sl.lum, light: b.light, edits: sl.edits + 1, relit: sl.relit + b.relit, denied: sl.denied} Slice

# --- edits ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Raise the column, and light it if what was placed is a torch.
fn buildUp({sl: Slice, c: int, y: int, kind: int}) -> Slice:
  var h = sl.height
  h[c] = y
  var lm = sl.lum
  lm[c] = if kind == 2: TORCH else: lm[c]
  return {height: h, lum: lm, light: sl.light, edits: sl.edits, relit: sl.relit, denied: sl.denied} Slice

# Placing is only legal on top of the existing column — the rule a real
# server enforces so a client cannot spawn geometry in mid-air. A refused
# edit is counted and costs no lighting work, which is the whole reason
# validation happens before the expensive part.
fn placeAt({sl: Slice, c: int, y: int, kind: int, cols: int}) -> Slice:
  if y != sl.height[c] + 1:
    return {height: sl.height, lum: sl.lum, light: sl.light, edits: sl.edits, relit: sl.relit, denied: sl.denied + 1} Slice
  let up = {sl: sl, c: c, y: y, kind: kind} buildUp
  return {sl: up, c: c, cols: cols} relight

# Taking the top block off takes any torch on that column with it — which is
# the expensive direction, because the light it was casting has to be
# unwound across the whole window rather than simply added to.
fn carve({sl: Slice, c: int}) -> Slice:
  var h = sl.height
  h[c] = h[c] - 1
  var lm = sl.lum
  lm[c] = 0
  return {height: h, lum: lm, light: sl.light, edits: sl.edits, relit: sl.relit, denied: sl.denied} Slice

# Bedrock is not diggable.
fn digAt({sl: Slice, c: int, cols: int}) -> Slice:
  if sl.height[c] <= 0:
    return {height: sl.height, lum: sl.lum, light: sl.light, edits: sl.edits, relit: sl.relit, denied: sl.denied + 1} Slice
  let cut = {sl: sl, c: c} carve
  return {sl: cut, c: c, cols: cols} relight

# `kind == 0` is a dig, 1 is stone, 2 is a torch. One message shape per
# shard rather than three: a zone server's wire protocol is one edit opcode,
# and it keeps the router's arms to a single send each.
fn applyEdit({sl: Slice, c: int, y: int, kind: int}) -> Slice:
  if kind == 0:
    return {sl: sl, c: c, cols: SPAN} digAt
  return {sl: sl, c: c, y: y, kind: kind, cols: SPAN} placeAt

# --- the log --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every shard reports to one Journal. This is the fan-IN half, and it is the
# stage a single-actor program never has: the Journal's mailbox takes one
# message per applied edit from four producers at once, so it is the first
# place in this corpus where two actors contend for one queue.

actor Journal [queue: 262144]:
  entries: int = 0
  chk:     int = 0
  relit:   int = 0
  denied:  int = 0
  shut:    int = 0

  # One entry per edit, carrying the column's resulting height — a change
  # log records the state an edit LEFT, which is what makes it replayable.
  #
  # `chk += h` and not a rolling hash, deliberately. A hash folds in arrival
  # ORDER, and the four shards interleave differently under every
  # `--actors:` mode; a sum is commutative, so it checks that the same edits
  # landed without also checking a sequence this program never promised.
  on logged({h: int}):
    entries += 1
    chk += h

  # A shard signs off exactly once, with its own totals. The barrier is the
  # COUNT, not a flag, and that is what makes it a real drain: the world is
  # closed when every shard has drained, and a shard cannot report until its
  # own mailbox has emptied down to the `flush` behind its edits.
  on closed({lit: int, bad: int}):
    relit += lit
    denied += bad
    shut += 1

fn worldDown() -> bool:
  return Journal.shut == SHARDS

# --- the shards -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Four singletons, written out. `sl = f(sl, ...)` is the natural spelling
# and also the fast one — it is the shape `movedCallInto` recognises, so the
# heightmap and the lightmap are MOVED into the handler rather than copied.
# Writing it through a local instead costs two full array copies per edit.

actor Shard0 [queue: 131072]:
  sl: Slice
  on start({n: int}):
    sl = {cols: SPAN} freshSlice
  on edit({c: int, y: int, kind: int}):
    sl = {sl: sl, c: c, y: y, kind: kind} applyEdit
    Journal send logged {h: sl.height[c]}
  on flush({n: int}):
    Journal send closed {lit: sl.relit, bad: sl.denied}

actor Shard1 [queue: 131072]:
  sl: Slice
  on start({n: int}):
    sl = {cols: SPAN} freshSlice
  on edit({c: int, y: int, kind: int}):
    sl = {sl: sl, c: c, y: y, kind: kind} applyEdit
    Journal send logged {h: sl.height[c]}
  on flush({n: int}):
    Journal send closed {lit: sl.relit, bad: sl.denied}

actor Shard2 [queue: 131072]:
  sl: Slice
  on start({n: int}):
    sl = {cols: SPAN} freshSlice
  on edit({c: int, y: int, kind: int}):
    sl = {sl: sl, c: c, y: y, kind: kind} applyEdit
    Journal send logged {h: sl.height[c]}
  on flush({n: int}):
    Journal send closed {lit: sl.relit, bad: sl.denied}

actor Shard3 [queue: 131072]:
  sl: Slice
  on start({n: int}):
    sl = {cols: SPAN} freshSlice
  on edit({c: int, y: int, kind: int}):
    sl = {sl: sl, c: c, y: y, kind: kind} applyEdit
    Journal send logged {h: sl.height[c]}
  on flush({n: int}):
    Journal send closed {lit: sl.relit, bad: sl.denied}

# The router. A `match` with one arm per shard, and it is a `match` because
# there is nothing to index: `Shard0` is a name, not a value, so no loop and
# no table can reach it. Adding a fifth zone server is an edit here.
fn toShard({s: int, c: int, y: int, kind: int}):
  match s:
    | 0 -> Shard0 send edit {c: c, y: y, kind: kind}
    | 1 -> Shard1 send edit {c: c, y: y, kind: kind}
    | 2 -> Shard2 send edit {c: c, y: y, kind: kind}
    | _ -> Shard3 send edit {c: c, y: y, kind: kind}

fn drainShards():
  Shard0 send flush {n: 0}
  Shard1 send flush {n: 0}
  Shard2 send flush {n: 0}
  Shard3 send flush {n: 0}

# --- the gateway ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Owns no world state, which is the point: it validates the coordinate and
# picks the owner. A gateway that needed the world would be a bottleneck in
# front of the shards and the sharding would buy nothing.

actor Gateway [queue: 262144]:
  routed:  int = 0
  bounced: int = 0

  # THE GATEWAY BRINGS ITS OWN SHARDS UP, and that is not tidiness — it is
  # the only spelling that is correct in every `--actors:` mode.
  #
  # `main` used to send `start` to the four shards itself. In `thread` mode
  # that works, because a send is visible the moment it is made and `start`
  # is made first. In `batch` mode a send is STAGED ON THE SENDING THREAD
  # and crosses when its batch fills, so main's four-message batch sat
  # unflushed while main pumped a hundred thousand sends at the gateway —
  # and the gateway's own edit batches, fat and flushing constantly,
  # reached Shard1 long before it. The shard indexed a `Slice` whose seqs
  # were still empty and died with `index 0 out of bounds for seq of length
  # 0`, at n=1000 and not at n=100.
  #
  # Tuck promises per-MAILBOX order, and a batch keeps it. What it does not
  # promise — and what thread mode hands you by accident — is any order
  # between two DIFFERENT senders to one mailbox. So a stage must be brought
  # up by whoever feeds it: `start` and `edit` now leave the same thread, in
  # that order, and no mode can separate them. Real deployments do this too,
  # for the same reason and not by analogy.
  on boot({n: int}):
    {} startWorld

  on edit({x: int, y: int, kind: int}):
    routed += 1
    let s = x /i SPAN
    let c = x % SPAN
    {s: s, c: c, y: y, kind: kind} toShard

  # `drain` arrives BEHIND every edit already routed, and FIFO per mailbox
  # is what makes that mean something: by the time the gateway sees it, it
  # has already forwarded everything ahead of it, so the flushes it sends
  # land behind those edits in each shard's queue. The barrier is correct
  # because of the ordering guarantee, not in spite of it.
  on drain({n: int}):
    {} drainShards

# --- the client feed ------------------------------------------------------
#
# ORDINARY CODE, not actors — the same point the matching engine makes.
# Actors are the processing half; getting bytes off a socket is an `[io]`
# task in a deployment and a plain loop here.

# A deterministic edit stream shaped like real play, which took two tries to
# get right and is worth writing down: the first version picked `x` and `y`
# independently at "random", and 88% of its edits were refused for landing
# at the wrong height. That is not a server under load, it is a server
# rejecting nonsense — and it measured the validation path instead of the
# lighting one.
#
# Players do not edit uniformly at random. They work ONE column in a burst,
# stacking blocks upward and then taking them back down, and they move on.
# Sixteen edits per burst: eight up, eight down, which leaves the column as
# it found it and makes the stream self-consistent however many times it
# revisits a column.
#
# Every ninth burst is a STALE CLIENT — one whose cached heightmap is out of
# date, so it aims three blocks too high and every edit in the burst is
# refused. That is the real source of rejections on a live server, and it
# keeps the validation path loaded at about 11% without drowning the work.
# Dig on the way back down; on the way up, every fourth block is a torch —
# players light a tunnel as they cut it.
fn blockKind({s: int}) -> int:
  if s >= 8:
    return 0
  return if s % 4 == 3: 2 else: 1

fn clientEdit({i: int}):
  let b = i /i 16
  let s = i % 16
  let x = (b * 37) % (SPAN * SHARDS)
  let kind = {s: s} blockKind
  let y = if b % 9 == 0: s + 3 else: s + 1
  Gateway send edit {x: x, y: y, kind: kind}

fn session({n: int}):
  var i = 0
  for i < n:
    {i: i} clientEdit
    i = i + 1

fn startWorld():
  Shard0 send start {n: 0}
  Shard1 send start {n: 0}
  Shard2 send start {n: 0}
  Shard3 send start {n: 0}

# PRINTED, not returned as an exit code — exit status is 8 bits and Unix has
# already spoken for much of it. Printing also makes the three `--actors:`
# modes and the three backends comparable for AGREEMENT: the shards are
# disjoint, so no edit's result depends on any other shard, and every mode
# must print the same numbers. A world server that answered differently per
# scheduler flag would be useless whatever its throughput.
fn report() -> void [io]:
  {text: "routed=" + Gateway.routed.toStr} printLine
  {text: "logged=" + Journal.entries.toStr} printLine
  {text: "relit=" + Journal.relit.toStr} printLine
  {text: "denied=" + Journal.denied.toStr} printLine
  {text: "world=" + Journal.chk.toStr} printLine

fn main() -> int [io]:
  Gateway send boot {n: 0}
  {n: 100000} session
  Gateway send drain {n: 0}
  Journal.waitUntil {pred: :worldDown}
  {} report
  return 0

# --- the seam, and what this program could not say ------------------------
#
# A shard relights only its OWN columns and stops one short of each end, so
# light does not cross a shard boundary: a torch at column 255 lights
# nothing in Shard1. That is a real distributed-systems problem and not an
# oversight — zone servers genuinely have this seam, and a real one handles
# it with a border exchange between neighbours after each tick.
#
# It is not written here because Tuck cannot express the interesting half.
# The exchange wants each shard to ask its NEIGHBOUR for a border strip, and
# a shard has no way to name a neighbour: there is no actor reference, so
# `Shard1` can only be written as the literal `Shard1`, and a shard that
# names its neighbours by hand is not sharded — it is four copies of a
# program that knows the whole topology at compile time. `toShard` above is
# already that compromise in miniature.
#
# The honest summary is that Tuck's actors model a fixed set of named
# services very well and a dynamic population of peers not at all, and the
# boundary between those two runs right through the middle of this file.
